    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esen-taishi.jpg" alt="Esen Taishi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ESENTAISH_KHAAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Avishay, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> were the leading tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a>, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, who had formed a loose confederation known as the Four Oirat after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> and in the fifteenth century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a>, briefly dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. In the early seventeenth century the Oirats were divided and under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>; some groups migrated west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where they became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, and others moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> as the Khoshut. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khara-khula">Khara Khula</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choros-oirats">Choros</a>, built up a power base in the steppe between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a>, and on his death in 1634 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> took the title of <em>khong taiji</em> and united most of the remaining Oirats. The name Dzungar, 'left hand', recalls their place on the left wing of the old Mongol army. In 1640 Erdeni Batur took part in a great assembly of Oirat and Khalkha princes that drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-oirat-code">Mongol-Oirat Code</a>, and he built a fortified settlement on the Khobuk River. After his death in 1653 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengge-dzungar-ruler">Sengge</a> succeeded him but was murdered in 1670, and Sengge's brother Galdan, who had studied as a Buddhist monk in Tibet, returned to take power, defeated his rivals and received the title Boshugtu Khan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a> around 1678.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashgar.jpg" alt="Kashgar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%ABytgah_Mosque,_Kashi_(20230923100109).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N509FZ, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> were pastoral nomads whose horses, sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> grazed the grasslands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> and the Ili valley, but their rulers worked deliberately to give the state a broader economic base. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> encouraged farming, and his successors settled thousands of Turkic-speaking Muslim farmers from the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in the Ili valley, where they grew grain for the nomads; their descendants became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranchi">Taranchi</a>. After 1680 the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altishahr">Altishahr</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> and Khotan, paid the Dzungars substantial tribute in grain, cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Trade linked the khanate with Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: Dzungar caravans sold horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and livestock in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a> and other Siberian towns and, after the peace of 1739, at Qing frontier markets, while Bukharan merchants handled much of the long-distance trade. The khans also developed mining and metalworking and produced armour, firearms and, with the help of Russian and Swedish prisoners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a>. Religion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaya-pandita">Zaya Pandita</a> created the Clear Script (<em>todo bichig</em>) in 1648 to write the Oirat language and translated many Buddhist texts into it, monasteries were built, and close ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> gave the khans religious legitimacy across the Mongol world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ulan-butung"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ulan-butung.jpg" alt="Battle of Ulan Butung" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ulan-butung">Battle of Ulan Butung</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qing_artillery_and_musketeers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">范承烈 (Fan Chenglie), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> army was a mounted force of nomad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, but from the late seventeenth century it also used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and, unusually for a steppe power, artillery. Galdan conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> in 1680, intervening on behalf of the Afaqi khojas, and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a>. In 1688 he invaded the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a>, whose princes fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ulan-butung">Battle of Ulan Butung</a> in 1690, a few hundred kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, Galdan's army sheltered behind a wall of kneeling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and escaped, but in 1696 Kangxi led a great expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and a Qing army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiyanggu">Fiyanggu</a> destroyed Galdan's forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jao-modo">Jao Modo</a>; Galdan died in 1697. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> (r. 1697–1727) rebuilt Dzungar power. In 1717 his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsering-dondub">Tsering Dondub</a> crossed the Tibetan plateau, killed the Khoshut ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhazang-khan">Lha-bzang Khan</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>; a Qing relief army was destroyed in 1718, but a second expedition expelled the Dzungars in 1720. From 1723 Tsewang Rabtan's forces drove the Kazakhs from the eastern steppe. The Swedish officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-gustaf-renat">Johan Gustaf Renat</a>, captured at Poltava, helped the Dzungars cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> in the 1720s. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a> the Dzungars destroyed a Qing army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hoton-nor">Hoton Nor</a> in 1731 but were defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a> in 1732, leading to the peace of 1739.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khanate was ruled by the Choros dynasty, whose heads used the title <em>khong taiji</em> or, like Galdan, khan; because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> rulers were not descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, the title of khan was conferred with the backing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. Power rested on the ruler's control of the <em>otog</em>, the basic units of herding households grouped under nobles, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> (<em>ulus</em>) held by princes of the ruling house. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-oirat-code">Mongol-Oirat Code</a> of 1640 regulated relations between princes, the status of the Buddhist clergy, military service, fines for crimes and the protection of property, and Galdan supplemented it with further decrees. Over time the khans built a more centralised state than most steppe powers: officials called <em>zaisang</em> administered the otog, special officials collected tribute from the oases, and the rulers kept a court in the Ili valley with workshops, granaries and Buddhist temples. Diplomacy was wide-ranging, with embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the Qing court, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the Central Asian khanates, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a> played Russia and the Qing against each other. Yet succession was never institutionalised, and almost every change of ruler, in 1670, after 1745 and again in the 1750s, brought murder or civil war among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choros-oirats">Choros</a> princes and their allies, which outside powers could exploit.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dolon-nor.jpg" alt="Dolon Nor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TTTC_Vol_I_033_Tolon-Noor.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Dzungar Khanate was the last great independent nomadic empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a>, and its contest with the Qing decided the political map of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. To defeat it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong</a> emperors built supply lines, granaries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-colony">military colonies</a> deep into the steppe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> wars drew the Qing into absorbing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, whose princes submitted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolon-nor">Dolon Nor</a> assembly in 1691, into establishing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> after 1720, and finally into conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, the 'new frontier'. At the same time Dzungar pressure pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> juzes into dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the Russian and Qing empires later divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> across the lands left open by the Dzungars' fall. The khanate's patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaya-pandita">Zaya Pandita</a>'s Clear Script left a lasting mark on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyk</a> culture, and the script remained in use among Kalmyks and Oirats into the twentieth century. The Dzungars are remembered very differently in different countries: as a menace in Kazakh history, as rebels in official Qing histories, and often as heroic defenders of Mongol independence in Mongolia. Their destruction is also one of the clearest cases of mass killing in early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and many modern historians describe it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren.jpg" alt="Galdan Tseren" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalmyk_warriors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Бобров, Леонид Александрович (*1980), CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren">Galdan Tseren</a>'s death in 1745 opened a decade of succession conflict. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-dorji-namjal">Tsewang Dorji Namjal</a> was overthrown and blinded in 1750 by his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lama-dorji">Lama Dorji</a>, who was himself killed in 1753 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawachi">Dawachi</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsering-dondub">Tsering Dondub</a>, with the help of the Khoit prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amursana">Amursana</a>. When Amursana fell out with Dawachi, he fled to the Qing in 1754, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> seized the opportunity. In 1755 two Qing armies, with Amursana among their commanders, marched to the Ili valley almost unopposed, and Dawachi was captured soon afterwards. Qianlong intended to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> into four khanates under Qing supervision, but Amursana, who had hoped to rule all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>, rebelled later in 1755 and was joined by many Oirat nobles. The emperor then ordered the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> as a people. Between 1755 and 1758 Qing troops and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> and Turkic allies killed large numbers of men, women and children, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> killed many more; Amursana fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and died of smallpox at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobolsk">Tobolsk</a> in 1757. Of a population usually estimated at around 600,000, the Qing scholar Wei Yuan reckoned that about 30 per cent were killed, 40 per cent died of smallpox and 20 per cent fled to Russia or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. Dzungaria was resettled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">bannermen</a>, Han and Hui colonists and Turkic farmers and became part of Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Dzungar Khanate was the last great nomadic empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> Mongol state that conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> steppe and challenged the Qing for seven decades, and whose destruction in 1755–1758 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> under Chinese rule and redrew the map of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</em></p>

